<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Final exam problems on hyperbolic functions
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-hyperbolic" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="chapter-hyperbolic-functions" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
A final exam problem on the hyperbolic identities. If you would like to review this topic first, see <xref ref="chapter-hyperbolic-functions" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-9-b">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
For any real number <m>x</m>, we have
<m>\cosh^2(x) + \sinh^2(x) = \cosh(2x)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
First,
<md xml:id="eq-rw23-cosh2x" number="yes">
\cosh(2x) = \frac12\left(e^{2x} + e^{-2x}\right).
</md>
Second,
<md xml:id="eq-rw23-sum" number="yes">
\cosh^2(x) + \sinh^2(x)
= \frac14\left(e^x + e^{-x}\right)^2
+ \frac14\left(e^x - e^{-x}\right)^2
= \frac14\left(2e^{2x} + 2e^{-2x}\right)
= \frac12\left(e^{2x} + e^{-2x}\right).
</md>
Comparing <xref ref="eq-rw23-cosh2x"/> and
<xref ref="eq-rw23-sum"/> gives
<m>\cosh^2(x) + \sinh^2(x) = \cosh(2x)</m>.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-1">
<statement>
<p>
Show that <m>\tanh^2(x) + \operatorname{sech}^2(x) = 1</m>, for all
real numbers <m>x</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We write both terms over the common denominator <m>\cosh^2(x)</m> and
use the identity <m>\cosh^2(x) - \sinh^2(x) = 1</m>, i.e.
<m>\sinh^2(x) + 1 = \cosh^2(x)</m>:
<md>
<mrow>\tanh^2(x) + \operatorname{sech}^2(x)
\amp = \frac{\sinh^2(x)}{\cosh^2(x)} + \frac{1}{\cosh^2(x)}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{\sinh^2(x) + 1}{\cosh^2(x)}
= \frac{\cosh^2(x)}{\cosh^2(x)}
= 1.</mrow>
</md>
Note that this is valid for every real <m>x</m>, since
<m>\cosh(x) \ge 1 \gt 0</m> and so the denominator is never zero.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-1">
<statement>
<p>
Compute <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\,3\sinh\!\left(\dfrac{3}{x}\right)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We use <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\sinh(u) = \cosh(u)\,\dfrac{du}{dx}</m> with
<m>u = \dfrac{3}{x} = 3x^{-1}</m>, so
<m>\dfrac{du}{dx} = -3x^{-2} = -\dfrac{3}{x^2}</m>. Hence
<md>
\frac{d}{dx}\,3\sinh\!\left(\frac3x\right)
= 3\cosh\!\left(\frac3x\right)\left(-\frac{3}{x^2}\right)
= -\frac{9}{x^2}\cosh\!\left(\frac3x\right).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-2">
<statement>
<p>
Simplify <m>\cosh(\ln 2)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Using the definition <m>\cosh x = \dfrac{e^x + e^{-x}}{2}</m> together
with <m>e^{\ln 2} = 2</m> and
<m>e^{-\ln 2} = \dfrac{1}{e^{\ln 2}} = \dfrac12</m>,
<md>
\cosh(\ln 2) = \frac{e^{\ln 2} + e^{-\ln 2}}{2}
= \frac{2 + \frac12}{2}
= \frac{\frac52}{2}
= \frac54.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
